Attribution
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Baton is a derivative work of the "handoff" skill from metaswarm:

    metaswarm — Multi-agent orchestration framework for Claude Code,
                Gemini CLI, and Codex CLI
    Author:   Dave Sifry
    Source:   https://github.com/dsifry/metaswarm
    License:  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Dave Sifry

The document structure, method steps, template, and anti-patterns originate
from metaswarm's handoff skill and are used under the terms of the MIT License.

Baton's additions over the original:
  - A pre-finalization Human Leverage Gate that pauses for one to three
    material human-owned choices, provides a recommendation, and records an
    option-preserving defer path; it repeats until clear without outsourcing
    discoverable or safely testable engineering judgment.
  - Section 0 "Receiving Session Contract" — records a long one-shot horizon,
    live-state boot checks, authority boundaries, hard stops, and the lightest
    adequate execution mechanism before work resumes.
  - A right-sizing ladder spanning direct execution, lightweight native
    delegation, structured orchestration, and Metaswarm only when persistent
    coordination or governance needs justify its overhead.
  - Compact, standard, and governed handoff depths so evidence remains
    proportional and narrow work does not inherit orchestration theater.
  - An evidence-led adversarial spine: classified claims, contradiction-seeking,
    fresh independent challenge, central spot-checks, and risk-scaled review.
  - Architecture and runnable walking-skeleton guidance before detailed feature
    expansion when substantial product work needs new structure.
  - Longest-safe-one-shot continuation rules rather than stopping automatically
    after one small milestone or maximizing fan-out.
  - Generalization to be framework-agnostic (no hard dependency on any
    orchestration framework, issue tracker, or plugin).

Grateful thanks to Dave Sifry and the metaswarm project.
